From 2adfa9293bf5b4ed5aa4e7a34cb6b980ffd77b46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Schaka <jonastanzer@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] clr: enable OpenCL support for gfx803 (Polaris)

Two independent CLR-layer blocks, on top of the ROCR-Runtime doorbell fix
(0001), also stand between gfx803 and a working OpenCL device:

1. device.hpp's Isa::runtimeRocSupported() unconditionally returns false
   for any ISA with versionMajor_ == 8 on the OpenCL (non-HIP) path,
   regardless of what the per-ISA runtimeRocSupported_ table already says
   -- gfx803's own table entry already carries runtimeRocSupported_=true;
   this extra check throws that away specifically for OpenCL. HIP is
   unaffected (IS_HIP short-circuits the check).

2. rocdevice.cpp's populateOCLDeviceConstants() queries a run of HSA
   image-extension attributes and returns false -- aborting device
   initialization entirely -- the moment any one query fails. These
   queries fail on gfx803 (the extension isn't populated for this legacy
   ISA), taking down the whole OpenCL device rather than just image
   support. Fixed by tracking query success in a local flag and disabling
   only image support (with a LogWarning) if any query failed, leaving
   every other OpenCL device capability to initialize normally.

Both confirmed via an AMD engineer's (lucbruni-amd) from-source TheRock
build verified on real Polaris hardware (RX 550) -- see
https://github.com/lucbruni-amd/TheRock/tree/lb/gfx803-polaris-support
and https://github.com/ROCm/clr/issues/269. Vendored into this
experimental pipeline per ROCm-gfx803's
patches/rocm-systems/opencl-gfx8-hardcoded-rejection.patch, which carries
the full investigation writeup this commit message only summarizes.

Needs 0001 applied first (or at least the same pinned commit) for the HSA
agent underneath to exist at all.
---
diff --git a/projects/clr/rocclr/device/device.hpp b/projects/clr/rocclr/device/device.hpp
index 06908d20e9..2a9961db5d 100644
--- a/projects/clr/rocclr/device/device.hpp
+++ b/projects/clr/rocclr/device/device.hpp
@@ -1477,9 +1477,6 @@ class Isa {

   /// @returns If the ROCm runtime supports the ISA.
   bool runtimeRocSupported() const {
-    if (!IS_HIP && (versionMajor_ == 8)) {
-      return false;
-    }
     return runtimeRocSupported_;
   }

